## 0. Core Invariants (never break)

> Architectural constraints this project must "never break." Changes that violate these are rejected outright.

{{INVARIANTS_BLOCK}}

## 1. Core Workflow

**After completing each operation, do these three things in order**:

1. **Update the docs first** — an entry under `docs/Progress.md` "## Changelog" (symptom / root cause / decision / verification result) is mandatory; a fix produced by a review-round rejection/re-review/final verification doesn't open a new entry — append one line ("review round N: …") to the end of the unit's existing entry instead; use the "## 2. Doc Sync Rules" table below to decide whether `docs/PLAN.md` (a Phase advanced / the plan changed), `docs/DECISIONS.md` (key decisions: tech choice, tradeoff, plan reversal), `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`, or `docs/DEPLOYMENT.md` also needs updating
2. **Make one atomic commit that includes both the code and the docs** — `git add` the source changes from this operation together with the doc updates from step 1; the message should make clear what changed and why (always review `git diff --staged` before committing)
3. **Push the current branch immediately** — `git push` the current feature/wip branch right after committing (no confirmation needed; use `-u` to set upstream if there isn't one yet; if there's no remote, follow §5.1 item 3 and remind the user to set one up)

The granularity of "one operation" ≈ the change covered by one atomic commit. Don't lump multiple independent changes into one big commit.

**Monthly changelog archiving**: the "Changelog" section of `docs/Progress.md` only keeps entries from the **current month**; at the first batch wrap-up of each month, cut last month's (and older) entries wholesale into `docs/archive/Progress-<YYYY-MM>.md` (split by the entry's own date, same format as the main file's changelog section, newest first; add a `# Progress Archive — <YYYY-MM>` heading line the first time the file is created), leaving one archive-index line under the main file's "Changelog" heading: `> Older entries are archived monthly in docs/archive/Progress-YYYY-MM.md`. The "Progress Overview" status table is never archived. The archiving action is its own separate `docs:` commit, never mixed with source changes. Reading rules are unchanged: only read the main file's last 2-3 entries when returning to the project; only go to the archive when tracing history.

> **Why "update docs first, then one commit" rather than "commit first, update docs after"** (this order flipped once before — this footnote exists so it doesn't flip again):
> 1. "Commit first, docs after" necessarily produces at least one historical commit where code and docs disagree; if the session gets interrupted right there, you're left in exactly the "wrote code but didn't write docs" end state that Section 5 explicitly forbids;
> 2. Updating the docs is part of the same "operation" — bundling it into one commit is what actually satisfies this section's own "one operation ≈ one atomic commit" rule; splitting it into two commits quietly redefines that granularity;
> 3. Empirical evidence: across multiple real-project smoke runs, docs and code were committed together throughout, and the resulting history stayed clean with no intermediate inconsistent state.

## 2. Doc Sync Rules

| Change type | Files that must be updated |
|---|---|
| New/modified source code | `docs/Progress.md` (implementation record) + `docs/PLAN.md` (phase progress) if needed |
| Bug fix / gotcha encountered | `docs/Progress.md` (symptom, root cause, fix) |
| Phase progresses | `docs/PLAN.md` + `docs/Progress.md` |
| `.env.example` / deploy script changes | `docs/Progress.md` + `docs/DEPLOYMENT.md` |
| Inter-module interface changes (HTTP API / WS payload) | `docs/Progress.md` + `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` (interface table) |
| Dependency changes (go.mod / package.json, etc.) | `docs/Progress.md` (what was added, why) |
| Requirement changes / reversals | `docs/REQUIREMENTS.md` (single source of truth, edit here first; division of labor note: business-fact changes go to BUSINESS, product-scope changes go to REQUIREMENTS) + `docs/Progress.md` |
| Business rule / business process changes | `docs/BUSINESS.md` (source of business facts, edit here first) + `docs/Progress.md` |
| Key decisions (tech choice / tradeoff / plan reversal / workaround) | `docs/DECISIONS.md` (What/Why/Changes) + `docs/Progress.md` |
| Meeting notes | `docs/MEETINGS.md` (raw archive) + `docs/BUSINESS.md` (distilled business facts) + `docs/REQUIREMENTS.md` (distilled product requirements) + `docs/REQUIREMENTS.md` Goal Ledger (requirement-type items) |
| A requirement/expectation/complaint surfaces in conversation | `docs/REQUIREMENTS.md` Goal Ledger (same turn, reply with one confirmation line) |
| Built a reusable component (general-purpose, not business-specific) | An entry in your org's component index (verify real paths first) + `docs/Progress.md` |
| `DECISIONS.inbox` has drafts | Triage before commit (distill decisions into `docs/DECISIONS.md`, requirements into the `docs/REQUIREMENTS.md` goal inbox, discard noise) |

**Principle**: docs are the single source of truth. When code and docs disagree, decide which one is correct, then bring the other into line.

## 3. Post-Change Quality Checks

1. Read through the changed file once to confirm the logic is correct
2. Grep related docs for stale info (old function names, field names, paths) and update them
3. Make sure code snippets / config examples in the docs match the actual code
4. UTF-8 mojibake scan: `LC_ALL=C grep -l $'\xef\xbf\xbd' docs/*.md .claude/*.md`
5. When a change affects `.env.example`, confirm the local `.env` (not in git) is synced too
6. After a frontend/interaction change, run a ui-sweep (interaction smoke walkthrough); once it's done, log one sweep record to `docs/Progress.md` (name `ui-sweep` explicitly, plus date + how many screens/clicks scanned + outcome — log it even with 0 defects); log real defects separately, one changelog entry each (symptom/root cause/fix); for destructive buttons outside the block list, extend the project's DENY_EXTRA first, then run it

## 4. Code Conventions

