## 5. Git Commit Conventions

- Commit message first line ≤ 72 characters, prefixed with `feat:` / `fix:` / `docs:` / `refactor:` / `test:` / `chore:`
- Any language is fine; keep the language consistent across commits
- Split large changes into atomic commits
- Always review `git diff --staged` before committing
- **Never commit**: `.env`, real credentials, `node_modules/`, `data/` (private), `*.db`
- **A conversation session must never end in a "wrote code but didn't write docs" state**

### 5.1 Branch & Backup Strategy

1. **Branch means push**: day-to-day development always happens on a feature/wip branch — switch to or create one before starting work (a worktree flow satisfies this automatically); push the current branch to the remote immediately after every commit, no confirmation needed — this is your real-time offsite backup
2. **main is gated — only for changes the user can see in the frontend**: before merging into main (or committing directly on main), ask one question — could the user see or test the difference from the frontend? **Yes** (frontend code changes, or backend logic changes that alter frontend interaction/display behavior) → get the user's confirmation first, attaching the changed page for inspection (merge all screenshots into one self-contained HTML — images embedded, opens directly in a browser, one caption line per image — deliver it in the conversation and print its local file path so it can be pasted into a browser; a text description alone or loose screenshots don't count; before delivering to the user, dispatch a visual-review subagent (`'opus'` + `'medium'`) to inspect every screenshot one by one, hunting the defects anyone would spot at a glance — broken layout, overlapping elements, overflowing or clipped text, mojibake or placeholder text, blank or data-less regions, obviously missing styles, error messages; fix what it finds, re-screenshot, re-review, and only deliver once the review passes; when unsure whether something is a defect or intentional design, don't force a fix — flag it in the reply for the user to decide, and include a one-line review verdict (pass, or what was fixed); if the app cannot be run or screenshots cannot be produced, stop and report the gate as blocked — never degrade to a text-only confirmation); **No** (anything the user can't test from the frontend: pure backend internals and refactors, docs, backend tests, scripts, CI, dependency bumps, changes that leave API behavior unchanged) → merge and push main directly, no confirmation needed — just report the merge in one line. Either way, delete the merged remote feature branch; when unsure, treat it as "yes" and ask; committing directly on main is still governed by rule 1 (branch first) — this clause only decides whether to ask. This clause overrides finishing-a-development-branch's Step 4 three-option menu — on a "no" verdict, skip the menu, merge, and continue the wrap-up
3. **No-remote fallback**: if the repo has no remote (or the branch has no upstream), remind the user to set one up after the first commit, so "auto-push" doesn't silently fail
4. **Delete worktrees when done**: only create them at `.worktrees/<branch-name>` inside the repo (must be in .gitignore) or the native EnterWorktree location `.claude/worktrees/`; never in a sibling directory (`xxx-wt-*`) or `/tmp`, including for one-off merge/audit uses; once merged, abandoned, or the one-off use ends, in the same wrap-up `git worktree remove` + `git worktree prune` + delete the local branch if it's merged into main, and say "worktrees cleaned" in the wrap-up report; clean up every worktree you created this session regardless of where it lives (this overrides finishing-a-development-branch's "only clean up under `.worktrees/`" limit); before wrapping up a batch, run `git worktree list` to audit and remove anything not part of the in-flight batch; leave alone (and list for the user) any worktree with uncommitted or unmerged content
5. **Squash review rounds into the unit commit**: a unit's first commit is `feat:`/`fix:` as usual; fixes produced afterward by review rejection, re-review, or final verification are committed with `git commit --squash=<the unit's first commit sha> -m "review round N: <what changed>"` (still push immediately after every commit as before — the backup habit doesn't change). Before merging into main, ahead of the gate decision, the main conversation runs `GIT_EDITOR=true git rebase --autosquash main` on the wip branch to squash each round into its unit commit (the round notes land automatically in the commit body), then `git push --force-with-lease origin <wip branch>`, then merges as usual. `--force-with-lease` is only allowed at this one step, only for its own wip branch — main and shared branches still forbid force push, unchanged, and the implementation agent's red line stands (this step is done by the main conversation at wrap-up, never dispatched). Fixes that come from real-machine/production feedback after a unit is already merged into main still get their own independent `fix:` commit as usual — that's genuine rework and should stay visible in the stats. Review rounds also don't open a new changelog entry in `docs/Progress.md`; they get appended as one line — "review round N: …" — at the end of that unit's existing entry

## 6. Secrets Handling

**Real values that must never be written into md files or code** (API keys, tokens, passwords, encryption keys, etc.) are all read from `.env` (not in git). `.env.example` uses placeholder values.

## 7. Planning & Doc Conventions (tied to brainstorming / ultracode)

- Design specs produced by brainstorming go to `docs/superpowers/specs/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>-design.md`
- **Once a spec is approved by the user, implement it directly from the spec via ultracode (Workflow multi-agent orchestration) — skip writing-plans / an implementation-plan document**
- Once a spec is approved, add a line under "## Spec Index" in `docs/PLAN.md`: `- [<topic>](superpowers/specs/<date>-<topic>-design.md) — <one-liner>`; PLAN.md stays a roadmap index and doesn't carry body content
- When writing-plans / subagent-driven / inline execution is explicitly named, follow the named approach
- Write specs, dispatch prompts, code comments, and commit messages for security/hardening work in neutral engineering language describing **what the system does** (validation, rate limiting, expiry, tightened permissions), not what attack it defends against; record threat scenarios in `docs/DECISIONS.md`, not in dispatch prompts or code comments — this avoids a content-safety classifier blanket-blocking the message and forcing a rewrite
- **Restate before acting**: for every requirement the user raises (a logic change, a new feature, a bug fix, an investigation — all count), once you understand it, the first line of your reply must be "Got it, next I'll do <a concrete one-line restatement>"; number them if there are several, one line each; fold this into the same line as the Goal Ledger log ("Got it, next I'll do X; logged to the Goal Ledger"), and only start reading files or making changes after the restatement; be concrete about the action and object ("fix the email-sending logic"), not just "Got it"; if you don't understand, ask directly rather than papering over it with a vague restatement
- **Multi-agent division of labor (applies to ultracode and any other parallel dispatch)**: the main conversation (strongest model, e.g. Fable/Opus) acts as Conductor — breaks down tasks, sets the approach, writes the Workflow script, does the final rollup and conflict adjudication; everything else is dispatched to subagents to execute. **Every stage's `model` and `effort` must be written explicitly** — omitting `model` inherits the main session's model, and omitting `effort` inherits the main session's tier (the main session often runs at xhigh, so omitting it means mechanical work runs at xhigh depth too):

  | Stage type | model | effort |
  |---|---|---|
  | Locating files / listing / inventory | `'haiku'` or `'sonnet'` | `'low'` |
  | Batch mechanical work: migration, renaming, templated code changes | `'sonnet'` | `'low'` |
  | Regular implementation (TDD units) | `'sonnet'` | `'medium'` |
  | Hard implementation: algorithms, concurrency, tricky bugs | `'sonnet'` | `'high'` |
  | Frontend visuals / UI polish ("does it look right" work; pure-logic frontend doesn't count) | `'opus'` | `'medium'` |
  | Blind round of a review chain (first round: 2–3 agents, none seeing the others, parallel, no vote counting) and dig rounds (carry every finding; refute first, then fill gaps); principle-3 units run every round at `'high'` | `'opus'` | `'medium'` |
  | Verdict round of a review chain (the last round; may be skipped when the blind round has nothing at P0/P1 and no contradictions; mandatory for principle-3 units): final review, acceptance verdict, security-related review | `'opus'` | `'high'` |
  | Architectural judgment, tradeoff decisions, final rollup | not dispatched, stays with the main conversation | — |

  - **Escalate on failure — don't pay up front**: implementation stages always start at `'medium'`; only escalate to `'high'` when a unit is bounced back by tests or review and rerun
  - **`'xhigh'` / `'max'` never go into batch stages** — reserve them for single-point judgments where "getting it wrong once is expensive"; if a review needs more confidence, add rounds rather than escalating a single round, and never count votes — blind-round agents don't see each other, start from zero and use fixed lenses (correctness; boundary and malformed data; security for the third), every dig round switches to a lens not used before (security, spec acceptance-criteria coverage, integration seams, rollback and reversibility, adversarial refutation), the tests themselves are checked every round, and no round rereads the same prompt; a same-lens round is a round that never happened
  - **Consequence overrides difficulty**: units touching data migrations, deleting/overwriting data, auth and permissions, payments, or public releases get `'opus'` + `'high'` on every round of the review chain (3 blind reviewers, dig rounds included) with a mandatory verdict round — a blind round alone may not pass them — no matter how low their implementation tier is, and the checklist must include "a rollback path exists and is executable"
  - Before entering ultracode, first output a 3-5 line kickoff summary (unit count, each unit's tier, estimated scale) — don't wait for confirmation, start right away; it just gives the user a visible interrupt window
  - Attach an opus review stage after every unit that writes code (stream them one by one via `pipeline`, don't fence-wait to line them all up); the review agent must actually read the changed files to verify — it must not just trust the implementer's report — and must also examine the tests themselves (do they cover the acceptance criteria; do they only test the happy path); weak tests count as a rejection, and a rejected unit's re-run splits tests and implementation across two agents
  - Review orchestration is always blind start + chained digging + verdict: the first round dispatches 2 review agents (3 for principle-3 high-risk units, every round at `'high'`) in parallel, none seeing the others, each from scratch on a fixed lens (correctness; boundary and malformed data; the third adds security; checking the tests themselves is done every round and takes no lens slot), with no vote counting — every agent returns the same schema findings[{file, line, severity: P0|P1|P2, claim, lens}], the script merges by file+line (repo-relative path, start line, ±3 lines count as the same spot; what can't be normalised goes to the next round) and marks same-location entries with opposite conclusions or different severities as conflict, semantic contradictions go to the next round; every later round is one agent whose prompt explicitly carries every earlier finding (confirmed items, overturned items, re-verified checkpoints), refutes first and then fills gaps on a lens not used before (in order: security, spec acceptance-criteria coverage, integration seams, rollback and reversibility, adversarial refutation), reporting only new findings and reversals; the last round is the verdict round `'opus'` + `'high'`, blind and dig rounds are `'medium'`. Routing: only P2 → pass and record as leftovers; nothing at P0/P1 but a conflict → no fix, straight to a verdict round; P1 → fix, then verdict round; P0 → fix, then one dig round, then verdict; a verdict round with new P0/P1 → fix and route the same way, counting toward the cap; stop when the verdict round has no new P0/P1 and nothing at P0/P1 is open (closed = fixed and confirmed by a later round), default cap 4 rounds (the blind round counts as one) and going past it needs a stated reason. Fixes never happen inside the review stage: as soon as a P0/P1 needs fixing this run is over for the unit, the main conversation dispatches implementation agents through the rejection flow and then starts a new run that continues the same chain, with the chain's state in `docs/reviews/<unit>-chain.md` (created and appended by the main conversation) whose path the next round's prompt receives and whose round count is inherited. Parallel multi-vote review (independent votes, each from zero, majority decides) is no longer used — every vote rediscovers the same problems and none can catch "the prior round's conclusion itself was wrong"; the blind round only discovers, it counts nothing and rules on nothing; diagnosis / root-cause / troubleshooting chain the same way, each round carrying the prior conclusions and rejected hypotheses, stop after two consecutive rounds with nothing new (same default cap of 4 rounds), no verdict round (unless the conclusion feeds an irreversible action)
  - Review reports state only the delta (new findings and reversals vs. the earlier rounds) — no "checked it, looks fine" summaries; when there's nothing new, say so explicitly and list the checkpoints that were re-verified
  - Beyond the spec excerpt and this file's hard rules, implementer-agent prompts must also name the production red lines and file ownership: the FORBIDDEN FILES list, never restart shared services, never read/write production data, no force push or any history rewrite that discards changes, and touch only the files assigned to this unit
  - A frontend-visual stage (`'opus'`) prompt first gives a design direction (cite the hallmark / frontend-design skill), then names the default styles the project's first pass actually produced so the agent avoids them, extending the list each round; generic "don't look AI-made" instructions do almost nothing on Opus 5.5
  - `effort` is only supported by Workflow's `agent()` — the bare Agent tool has no such parameter → prefer Workflow orchestration for batch mechanical work
  - Pure-mechanical stages (locating/listing; batch migration/renaming/templating) dispatch via `agent(..., { agentType: 'mechanical', effort: 'low' })` — `mechanical` is a subagent shipped in this plugin with `omitClaudeMd: true`, so it skips the auto-loaded CLAUDE.md and saves tokens; because it doesn't load CLAUDE.md, any write-capable mechanical stage must carry the production red lines in its own prompt. Regular implementation and all review agents keep CLAUDE.md.
  - The Workflow per-run concurrent-agent cap defaults to about `min(16, cores-2)`; raise it with the env var `CLAUDE_CODE_WORKFLOW_MAX_CONCURRENT_AGENTS` (1–256) only when the machine has CPU/memory headroom, never on an already-loaded machine
  - The tier table is a dated empirical calibration — after each major model generation, revisit it against your session usage data; change the table based on data, not vibes

## Never Do

- Change code without updating docs / committing
- Write real credentials into md files
- Commit `data/` into git
- Break the Core Invariants in Section 0
- Merge a change the user can see in the frontend into main/the default branch (via merge or a direct commit) without confirmation — changes with no frontend-visible difference merge directly per §5.1, no asking
- Hear a requirement and not log it to the Goal Ledger (not logging it counts as not having heard it; when unsure, log it as `open` anyway — better to over-log than to miss one)
- Hear a requirement and start work without restating it first (the first line must be "Got it, next I'll do X")
- Leave a worktree undeleted when done (once merged/abandoned, remove + prune + delete the local branch in the same wrap-up; never create one in a sibling directory or `/tmp`)
- Give a review-round fix its own independent `fix:` commit while the unit hasn't merged into main yet (use `git commit --squash=<unit's first commit sha>` instead; independent `fix:` commits are only for post-merge production feedback)
- Let `docs/Progress.md`'s changelog pile up past the current month unarchived (archive last month's entries into `docs/archive/Progress-<YYYY-MM>.md` at the first batch wrap-up of each month)

